per supplies that mechanism. Using the same toy-model setup as 4 , we show that adversarially trained models represent systematically fewer features than standardly trained ones, and that the discarded features are exactly the ones 5 ’s taxonomy would classify as non-robust. We establish this first indirectly, through interference geometry and represented feature count, and then directly, using a synthetic dataset in which each feature’s robust/non-robust identity is assigned in advance, rather than inferred from model behavior after training. Our contributions are three-fold: • We show that adversarially trained models consistently drop more features than standardly trained models, across our sparsity sweep. • We show that dropped features carry higher average interference than retained features, and that retained features cluster near antipodal interference ( ≈ − 1 \approx-1 ), the configuration that minimizes cross-feature interference. • Using a synthetic dataset with a known ground-truth partition, we show that the dropped features correspond exactly to the non-robust feature set. 2 Background 2.1 Superposition Superposition occurs when a model encodes more features than there are neurons ( 2 ) . We control it by intervening on sparsity when training toy models, following the setup of 2 . Sparsity is governed by a threshold S ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] S\in[0,1] , which determines the activity of each component of the input x ∈ ℝ m x\in\mathbb{R}^{m} : [ x ] i = { x i if ​ x i > S 0 otherwise [x]_{i}= egin{cases}x_{i}&	ext{if }x_{i}>S\ 0&	ext{otherwise}\end{cases} A feature x i x_{i} is therefore active with probability p ⁡ ( A ) = 1 − S p(A)=1-S . 2.2 Interference 2 define interference as the geometry of features in activation space, measured by the degree of non-orthogonality between feature directions. For two feature directions W : , i , W : , j ∈ ℝ n W_{:,i},W_{:,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{n} , they are orthogonal when W : , i ⊤ W : , j = 0 W_{:,i}^{	op}W_{:,j}=0 , and exhibit int